specific information in written texts about the different social and work contexts.", "type": "secondary_8"}]}, {"type": "learning_points", "contents": [{"content": "Using Homonyms and Homophones", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "Homonyms are made up of homophones and homographs.", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "<span class=\"strong\">Homophones </span>are words that sound the same but have different meanings. They may be spelled the same or differently.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "For example:<br/> <span class=\"italic\">right </span>and <span class=\"italic\">write</span><br/> <span class=\"italic\">right </span>(noun) and <span class=\"italic\">right </span>(adjective)", "type": "text"}, {"content": "<span class=\"strong\">Homographs </span>are words are spelled the same, but are pronounced differently. They also have different meanings.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "For example:<br/><span class=\"italic\">present </span>(noun) and <span class=\"italic\">present </span>(verb)<br/><span class=\"italic\">wind </span>(noun) and <span class=\"italic\">wind </span>(verb)", "type": "text"}, {"content": "their ...  there", "type": "example"}, {"content": "your ... you're", "type": "example"}, {"content": "phased ... fazed", "type": "example"}, {"content": "sell ... cell", "type": "example"}, {"content": "pair ... pear", "type": "example"}, {"content": "pre<span class=\"strong\">sent</span> ... <span class=\"strong\">pre</span>sent", "type": "example"}]}, {"type": "learning_points", "contents": [{"content": "Intonation of Short Questions, Statements and Question Tags", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "Intonation is the rise and fall of your voice when you speak. You use intonation to convey meaning. It is the speaker's choice.", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "In short statements, you use falling intonation to signal that you are finished what you are saying.<br/> <span class=\"italic\">No, they aren't.</span>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "In short questions, the intonation patterns are:", "type": "text"}, {"content": "<span class=\"strong\">Yes/No Question</span> = Rising Intonation <br/><span class=\"italic\">Have you talked to any of the others?</span>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "<span class=\"strong\">WH/Information Question</span> = Falling Intonation <br/> <span class=\"italic\">How are you doing?</span>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "In <span class=\"strong\">Tag Questions</span> (short questions at the end of statements) = Rising or Falling Intonation. You use <span class=\"strong\">falling intonation</span> (your voice goes down) to signal that you believe what you said is correct. ", "type": "text"}, {"content": "You use <span class=\"strong\">rising intonation</span> (your voice goes up) when you are checking, because you are not sure if you are correct.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "These two sentences are the same, but they have slightly different meanings as they are said differently, depending on the speaker's intention. ", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Examples: <br/><span class=\"italic\">It isn't right, is it?  <br/>It is so ridiculous, isn't it?</span>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "You are looking for other work now, aren't you?", "type": "example"}, {"content": "Have you spoken to them?", "type": "example"}, {"content": "You have talked to some companies, haven't you?", "type": "example"}, {"content": "You're angry, aren't you?", "type": "example"}, {"content": "There are going to be interesting times ahead, aren't there?", "type": "example"}]}, {"type": "takeaway_extension", "contents": [{"content": "In pairs, research 5 homonyms. ", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Understand the meaning and be able to give an example.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "After, separate and make groups of three or four.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Each person makes a brief presentation of their pair's homonyms.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "The other group members write them down.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Reunite the original pairs and compare the different homonyms you have learned.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Finally, select 5 homonyms that you like the best and write a short paragraph.", "type": "text"}]}, {"type": "takeaway_assessment", "contents": [{"content": "I research homonyms.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "I explain homonyms and give examples.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "I write a short paragraph using homonyms.", "type": "text"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "long run", "type": "name"}, {"content": "after a long time", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: In the long run they lose money because they have inexperienced staff which will cost them customers.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn9le09ob04aud01", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word_describer", "contents": [{"content": "These are words you use when discussing job prospects.", "type": "text"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "abhorrent", "type": "name"}, {"content": "detestable", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(adjective)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: It is abhorrent how our former company treated us by keeping on scab workers and phasing out the older workers.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn9le09ob04aud02", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "aberrant", "type": "name"}, {"content": "abnormal", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(adjective)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: Their management style is completely aberrant.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn9le09ob04aud03", "type": "audio"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"content": "to phase out", "type": "name"}, {"content": "to stop using something or someone gradually or in stages", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(verb)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: It is abhorrent how our former company treated us by kee